Third-party material in the real-world conformance corpus (sweep_realworld_*.py)
===============================================================================

1. tldr-pages
-------------

The `real tldr ...` cases in sweep_realworld_text.py, sweep_realworld_files.py,
sweep_realworld_misc.py and sweep_realworld_builtins.py are adapted from the tldr-pages
project:

    tldr-pages, https://github.com/tldr-pages/tldr
    Copyright (c) 2014-present the tldr-pages team and contributors
    Licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0),
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Revision used: 8cf22035e60b167ebf224c1c527b27e0b5dca24d (pages/common and pages/linux)

What was changed:

- Only the example commands were used, for the commands and builtins this shell provides.
  The descriptions of the examples were shortened into case names; no page text is copied.
- Every {{placeholder}} was bound to a concrete value or to a fixture file that the case's
  script creates first (for example `{{path/to/file}}` became `input.txt` holding generated
  text). Alternatives such as `{{[-n|--lines]}}` became one case per spelling.
- Examples were adapted so that they run without a terminal and deterministically: dates are
  pinned with `-d` and `TZ=UTC`, sleeps are shortened, random output is sorted or checked only
  for its shape, output whose order is the filesystem's is sorted, `/etc/passwd` became a
  fixture file, long listings (`ls -l`, plain `stat`) were dropped, and commands the shell does
  not have (gzip, du, tar, sudo) were replaced by ones it has.
- Each example was multiplied over input shapes (empty input, no final newline, CRLF line
  ends, multibyte UTF-8, blank lines, an embedded NUL, a missing file, a directory operand) and
  invocation forms (file operand, `-` on a pipe, redirected stdin).
- Examples that need the network, a real home directory, other users, permissions, awk, a
  terminal or interactive programs were left out.

2. Coding-agent trajectories
----------------------------

The `real traj: ...` cases in sweep_realworld_agents.py replay the shapes of shell commands
found in two public datasets of coding-agent trajectories:

    SWE-bench/SWE-smith-trajectories, https://huggingface.co/datasets/SWE-bench/SWE-smith-trajectories
    Licensed under the MIT License (dataset card: `license: mit`)
    Revision used: 08e109b4a59eaeebf80e4675cd125d42e7ac99a4, split `tool`,
    rows 0-99, 3000-3099, 6000-6099, 9000-9099, 12000-12099, 15000-15099, 18000-18099, 21000-21099

    SWE-Gym/OpenHands-SFT-Trajectories, https://huggingface.co/datasets/SWE-Gym/OpenHands-SFT-Trajectories
    Licensed under the MIT License (dataset card: `license: mit`)
    Revision used: 4aaa5a4a4b5861f4799d2336908760c190ac3b17, split `train.success.oss`, all rows

What was changed:

- About 4,500 shell commands were extracted from the trajectories' tool calls and grouped by
  shape: the commands, their options and the pipeline structure, with paths, patterns and
  numbers abstracted. Shapes that need tools this shell does not have (python, pip, git, ps,
  chmod, awk) were dropped.
- Each remaining shape was rewritten against an invented Python project that the case creates
  under /testbed. Repository names, file paths, identifiers, search patterns and file contents
  from the trajectories were all replaced; no code, data, prompts or other text from the
  datasets is included, and nothing private (secrets, host names, e-mail addresses, personal
  paths) was carried over.

